<main class="methodology-page"><div class="container narrow-layout"><span class="eyebrow">Editorial methodology</span><h1>How PetHomeScout evaluates a pet-home decision.</h1><p class="lead">We distinguish research-led guidance from founder-tested observations. Every score needs a visible source and a review date.</p><div class="method-grid"><section><b>1</b><h2>Set up the household context</h2><p>We record product setup, pet type, hair load, flooring, room layout, and the job the product is expected to do.</p></section><section><b>2</b><h2>Log the observable outcome</h2><p>We capture what worked, what created friction, what we could not verify, and the evidence date.</p></section><section><b>3</b><h2>Explain the limitations</h2><p>We do not turn a single household result into a promise for every pet or home.</p></section></div><section class="rubric-panel"><h2>Founder-led test rubric</h2><dl><dt>Setup &amp; learning curve</dt><dd>What is required before the item works reliably?</dd><dt>Pet-home context</dt><dd>Pet type, hair characteristics, floor type, and room conditions.</dd><dt>Observed results</dt><dd>What was observed, when, and under which conditions?</dd><dt>Ownership friction</dt><dd>Maintenance, consumables, noise, cleaning, and recurring work.</dd><dt>Limitations</dt><dd>What remains unknown or varies by household?</dd></dl></section><div class="disclosure-note"><strong>Editorial independence:</strong> a commercial relationship never changes an evidence status or score. Future merchant links will be disclosed where they appear.</div></div></main>
